\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Background symmetries form a presheaf of groupoids}
\date{2026-11-03}

\p{Let a group #{G} act on the left on a set #{X}. For each subgroup
#{H}, the action groupoid #{H\ltimes X} has the elements of #{X} as
objects, and an arrow #{h:x\to y} for each #{h\in H} with
#{h\cdot x=y}. If #{H} is contained in a subgroup #{K}, sending
each arrow to itself is a functor
#{H\ltimes X\to K\ltimes X}, identical on objects. These functors
preserve identities and composition, and they compose with the
inclusions of subgroups. The assignment is therefore a functor from
the poset of subgroups of #{G} to groupoids. Equivalently, it is a
groupoid-valued presheaf on the opposite of that poset.}

\p{The isomorphism classes of #{H\ltimes X} are the orbits of #{H}.
Thus the objects modulo the symmetries in #{H} are the isomorphism
classes of that groupoid. The [enriched four-cycle
backgrounds](fdip-000X) carry an action of the dihedral group
constructed there. The action groupoid in that note is the value of
this presheaf at that whole group. That note is transcluded by
[enriched backgrounds](fdip-001E). The [reader guide](fdip-004E)
places that comparison in Part V, with the Clifford algebras and
the Dirac operators. The chapter on [backgrounds and
symmetry](fdip-0019) does not transclude the four-cycle note or
the enriched-backgrounds note. It says that its examples prepare
those comparisons. The arrow pattern is the
[action groupoid](fgap-000P). This note does not claim that every
background in that chapter arises from the four-cycle.}

\p{\em{No stack is proved.} A stack would require a topology and a
descent equivalence for compatible families. No cover is defined
here, and no descent map is shown to be an equivalence. The finite
tests of [where the topos layer begins](fgap-000U) record that the
action-groupoid example does not require a quotient stack, and that
those tests have supplied no site, descent theorem, or physical
correspondence.
\em{Whether this presheaf organises a stack of backgrounds remains
open.}}

\proof{
\p{Composition in #{H\ltimes X} sends #{g:x\to y} and
#{k:y\to z} to #{kg:x\to z}. The action law gives
#{(kg)\cdot x=k\cdot(g\cdot x)}, so the composite is an arrow.
The identity arrow is #{1:x\to x}. The inverse of #{h:x\to h\cdot x}
is #{h^{-1}:h\cdot x\to x}, and #{h^{-1}} lies in #{H} because #{H}
is a subgroup. The two inverse identities and associativity are the
corresponding group laws. Every arrow is therefore invertible.}

\p{Suppose #{H\subset K}. An arrow labelled by an element of #{H}
is an arrow of #{K\ltimes X} with the same source and target. The
identity element and the product #{kg} are unchanged by this
inclusion, so the map on arrows preserves identities and
composition. If #{H\subset K\subset L}, the composite of the two
inclusions of arrows is the inclusion of #{H} into #{L}. The
inclusion of a subgroup into itself is the identity functor. This
is a functor from the subgroup poset to groupoids. That poset
has one morphism from #{H} to #{K} exactly when #{H} is contained
in #{K}, and the functor sends it to the inclusion of action
groupoids. A
groupoid-valued presheaf on a category is a functor from the
opposite of that category. The opposite of the opposite of the
subgroup poset is the subgroup poset, so the functor just
constructed is a groupoid-valued presheaf on the opposite poset.}

\p{Two objects are isomorphic exactly when some element of #{H}
carries one to the other, which is the definition of an orbit.
For the dihedral action, the groupoid at the whole group is the
one whose arrows are already identified in the enriched four-cycle
note. No topology on the subgroup poset is defined, so the
presheaf is not given the data of a stack.}
}
